WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.056 --> 00:26.526 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference May 1,

00:26.526 --> 00:29.356 align:middle
2019 CHAIR POWELL.

00:29.356 --> 00:41.546 align:middle
Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:41.546 --> 01:37.316 align:middle
At the FOMC meeting
that concluded today,

01:37.316 --> 01:50.626 align:middle
we reviewed economic and
financial developments

01:50.626 --> 02:07.606 align:middle
in the United States and
around the world and decided

02:07.606 --> 02:17.936 align:middle
to leave our policy
interest rate unchanged.

02:17.936 --> 02:33.896 align:middle
My colleagues and I have
one overarching goal:

02:33.896 --> 02:52.776 align:middle
to use our monetary policy tools

02:58.456 --> 03:03.516 align:middle
to sustain the economic
expansion

03:03.826 --> 03:10.666 align:middle
with a strong job market and
stable prices for the benefit

03:10.786 --> 03:14.776 align:middle
of the American people.

03:14.776 --> 03:18.686 align:middle
Incoming data since
our last meeting

03:18.686 --> 03:24.926 align:middle
in March have been broadly in
line with our expectations.

03:26.336 --> 03:32.506 align:middle
Economic growth and job creation
have both been a bit stronger

03:32.506 --> 03:33.796 align:middle
than we anticipated,

03:33.846 --> 03:37.156 align:middle
while inflation has
been somewhat weaker.

03:37.316 --> 03:42.226 align:middle
Overall, the economy
continues on a healthy path,

03:42.576 --> 03:44.436 align:middle
and the Committee believes
that the current stance

03:44.436 --> 03:45.826 align:middle
of policy is appropriate.

03:46.376 --> 03:49.146 align:middle
The Committee also believes

03:49.146 --> 03:51.746 align:middle
that solid underlying
fundamentals are supporting the

03:51.746 --> 03:54.496 align:middle
economy, including accommodative
financial conditions,

03:55.036 --> 03:58.406 align:middle
high employment and job
growth, rising wages,

03:58.886 --> 04:01.046 align:middle
and strong consumer
and business sentiment.

04:02.276 --> 04:05.496 align:middle
Job gains rebounded in March
after a weak reading in February

04:05.596 --> 04:08.456 align:middle
and averaged 180,000 per
month in the first quarter,

04:09.126 --> 04:10.266 align:middle
well above the pace needed

04:10.266 --> 04:12.436 align:middle
to absorb new entrants
to the labor force.

04:13.896 --> 04:16.046 align:middle
Although first-quarter
GDP rose more

04:16.046 --> 04:17.826 align:middle
than most forecasters
had expected,

04:18.386 --> 04:19.806 align:middle
growth in private consumption

04:19.806 --> 04:21.486 align:middle
and business fixed
investment slowed.

04:22.606 --> 04:25.486 align:middle
Recent data suggest that these
two components will bounce back,

04:25.676 --> 04:29.066 align:middle
supporting our expectation
of healthy GDP growth

04:29.146 --> 04:30.116 align:middle
over the rest of the year.

04:31.906 --> 04:33.446 align:middle
The Committee is
strongly committed

04:33.446 --> 04:35.886 align:middle
to our symmetric 2 percent
inflation objective.

04:37.546 --> 04:39.006 align:middle
For much of this long expansion,

04:39.006 --> 04:41.456 align:middle
inflation ran a bit below
our 2 percent objective,

04:41.926 --> 04:45.006 align:middle
alongside considerable slack
in resource utilization.

04:46.386 --> 04:48.446 align:middle
But last year, with the
unemployment rate at

04:48.446 --> 04:50.586 align:middle
or below 4 percent,
inflation moved up.

04:51.506 --> 04:52.636 align:middle
From March through December,

04:52.756 --> 04:55.266 align:middle
core inflation-which
excludes volatile food

04:55.266 --> 04:58.906 align:middle
and energy components-was at
or very close to 2 percent.

05:00.256 --> 05:03.946 align:middle
Overall inflation
fluctuated from a few tenths

05:03.976 --> 05:06.916 align:middle
above 2 percent to a few
tenths below over this period,

05:07.486 --> 05:10.656 align:middle
with the moves mostly due
to changes in energy prices.

05:11.736 --> 05:15.026 align:middle
As expected, overall inflation
fell at the start of this year

05:15.026 --> 05:20.056 align:middle
as earlier oil price declines
worked through the system.

05:20.276 --> 05:22.336 align:middle
Overall inflation for
the 12 months ended

05:22.336 --> 05:24.266 align:middle
in March was 1.5 percent.

05:25.496 --> 05:28.266 align:middle
Core inflation unexpectedly
fell as well, however,

05:28.446 --> 05:30.636 align:middle
and as of March stood
at 1.6 percent

05:30.696 --> 05:31.866 align:middle
for the previous 12 months.

05:32.766 --> 05:35.626 align:middle
We suspect that some transitory
factors may be at work.

05:36.366 --> 05:40.406 align:middle
Thus, our baseline view remains
that, with a strong job market

05:40.406 --> 05:43.176 align:middle
and continued growth, inflation
will return to 2 percent

05:43.176 --> 05:45.896 align:middle
over time and then
be roughly symmetric

05:45.896 --> 05:47.666 align:middle
around our longer-term
objective.

05:49.476 --> 05:50.286 align:middle
At the start of the year,

05:50.516 --> 05:53.816 align:middle
a number of crosscurrents
presented risks to the outlook,

05:53.876 --> 05:57.046 align:middle
including weak global
growth, particularly in China

05:57.046 --> 06:00.036 align:middle
and Europe; the possibility
of a disruptive Brexit;

06:00.786 --> 06:03.886 align:middle
and uncertainty around
unresolved trade negotiations.

06:05.106 --> 06:07.066 align:middle
While concerns remain
in all of these areas,

06:07.406 --> 06:09.616 align:middle
it appears that risks
have moderated somewhat.

06:10.996 --> 06:14.206 align:middle
Global financial conditions have
eased, supported in many places

06:14.206 --> 06:16.456 align:middle
around the world by
an accommodative shift

06:16.456 --> 06:19.646 align:middle
in monetary policy and, in
some cases, fiscal policy.

06:20.976 --> 06:23.836 align:middle
Recent data from China and
Europe show some improvement,

06:25.046 --> 06:27.736 align:middle
and the prospect of a disorderly
Brexit has been pushed off

06:27.736 --> 06:28.136 align:middle
for now.

06:29.746 --> 06:32.466 align:middle
Further, there are reports of
progress in the trade talks

06:32.466 --> 06:34.016 align:middle
between the United
States and China.

06:35.176 --> 06:36.866 align:middle
The Committee views
these developments,

06:36.906 --> 06:38.806 align:middle
along with the outlook
for continued growth,

06:39.296 --> 06:42.006 align:middle
a strong job market, and
muted inflation pressures,

06:42.496 --> 06:44.476 align:middle
as consistent with
continued patience

06:44.546 --> 06:47.146 align:middle
in assessing further
adjustments in monetary policy.

06:49.256 --> 06:51.126 align:middle
Over the past several
months, we have made a number

06:51.126 --> 06:53.236 align:middle
of consequential decisions
about our balance sheet.

06:53.726 --> 06:54.916 align:middle
In January, we decided

06:54.916 --> 06:57.826 align:middle
to continue implementing
monetary policy using our

06:58.056 --> 06:59.246 align:middle
current policy regime,

06:59.776 --> 07:02.806 align:middle
which involves providing an
ample supply of reserves.

07:03.726 --> 07:06.766 align:middle
In March, we decided to slow the
pace of balance sheet runoff,

07:06.766 --> 07:07.706 align:middle
starting this month,

07:08.186 --> 07:10.536 align:middle
and to cease runoff
entirely in September.

07:11.646 --> 07:14.566 align:middle
These plans support our
longer-run dual-mandate

07:14.566 --> 07:17.836 align:middle
objectives and also provide
clarity about the path

07:17.876 --> 07:19.056 align:middle
of our asset holdings.

07:20.666 --> 07:22.546 align:middle
Today we had a preliminary
discussion

07:22.546 --> 07:25.436 align:middle
about the longer-run maturity
composition of the portfolio.

07:26.446 --> 07:27.846 align:middle
Before the financial crisis,

07:27.846 --> 07:30.626 align:middle
our portfolio was weighted
toward shorter-term debt

07:30.626 --> 07:31.486 align:middle
of the federal government.

07:33.026 --> 07:35.516 align:middle
In the wake of the crisis,
the Fed bought a large amount

07:35.516 --> 07:37.666 align:middle
of longer-term securities
with the aim

07:37.666 --> 07:40.036 align:middle
of lowering longer-term
interest rates and, thus,

07:40.036 --> 07:41.146 align:middle
supporting the recovery.

07:42.616 --> 07:43.786 align:middle
Because of these purchases,

07:43.826 --> 07:47.366 align:middle
our portfolio is now weighted
toward longer-term securities.

07:48.486 --> 07:50.256 align:middle
As part of normalization,
we will have

07:50.256 --> 07:54.466 align:middle
to decide what the
maturity structure should be

07:54.466 --> 07:55.346 align:middle
in the longer term.

07:56.356 --> 07:58.776 align:middle
This choice raises
many complex issues

07:58.776 --> 08:01.536 align:middle
and has possible implications
for the stance of policy.

08:03.196 --> 08:05.746 align:middle
Today's preliminary
discussion laid the groundwork

08:05.746 --> 08:07.636 align:middle
for more complete
analysis and discussion,

08:07.916 --> 08:08.976 align:middle
and we plan to return

08:08.976 --> 08:11.276 align:middle
to the maturity composition
question toward the end

08:11.276 --> 08:11.706 align:middle
of the year.

08:12.576 --> 08:16.016 align:middle
There is no pressing need to
resolve this matter, however,

08:16.416 --> 08:19.016 align:middle
and any decisions we ultimately
reach will be implemented

08:19.016 --> 08:22.306 align:middle
with considerable advance notice
and in a manner that allows

08:22.306 --> 08:23.396 align:middle
for smooth adjustment.

08:23.396 --> 08:26.216 align:middle
As we have often emphasized,

08:26.296 --> 08:29.126 align:middle
adjustments to the balance sheet
normalization process may well

08:29.126 --> 08:31.146 align:middle
be needed as the
process unfolds.

08:32.686 --> 08:34.996 align:middle
Finally, we made a small
technical adjustment in one

08:34.996 --> 08:37.416 align:middle
of our tools for
implementing monetary policy:

08:37.416 --> 08:40.906 align:middle
the interest rate on excess
reserves, or IOER rate.

08:41.906 --> 08:44.046 align:middle
The change does not
reflect any shift

08:44.136 --> 08:46.266 align:middle
in the intended stance
of monetary policy.

08:46.426 --> 08:49.586 align:middle
We use the IOER rate to help
keep the federal funds rate

08:49.586 --> 08:50.566 align:middle
in our target range.

08:51.186 --> 08:53.896 align:middle
As balance sheet normalization
continues, we have expected

08:54.256 --> 08:56.256 align:middle
that the effective federal
funds rate would shift

08:56.256 --> 08:58.546 align:middle
up over time relative
to the IOER rate.

08:59.406 --> 09:01.716 align:middle
Last year we twice
lowered the IOER rate

09:01.716 --> 09:04.796 align:middle
by 5 basis points relative to
the top of the target range

09:05.236 --> 09:07.646 align:middle
after the federal funds
rate moved toward the top

09:07.646 --> 09:08.256 align:middle
of the range.

09:08.916 --> 09:11.556 align:middle
These actions helped keep the
effective federal funds rate

09:11.696 --> 09:13.016 align:middle
well within the target range.

09:13.656 --> 09:15.936 align:middle
And today we made
one more such change.

09:16.646 --> 09:19.206 align:middle
The target range for the federal
funds rate is our main indicator

09:19.206 --> 09:21.786 align:middle
of the stance of policy,
and it remains unchanged.

09:23.156 --> 09:23.926 align:middle
Thank you very much.

09:23.926 --> 09:25.946 align:middle
I will be glad to
take your questions.

09:26.696 --> 09:29.386 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:30.026 --> 09:30.976 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

09:30.976 --> 09:33.736 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

09:33.736 --> 09:34.806 align:middle
As the statement noted,

09:34.886 --> 09:37.286 align:middle
core inflation is now running
below 2 percent-it's been

09:37.806 --> 09:39.306 align:middle
falling for three
straight months,

09:39.306 --> 09:43.666 align:middle
and while you've been close,
it's only been at 2 percent

09:43.666 --> 09:46.246 align:middle
or above one month since 2012.

09:46.746 --> 09:52.086 align:middle
Mr. Chair, I guess I wonder, is
it time to address low inflation

09:52.086 --> 09:54.436 align:middle
through policy, and can you give
us some sense of your metric

09:54.486 --> 09:56.246 align:middle
for when it would be time?

09:56.246 --> 09:59.676 align:middle
At what level would it
require a policy response

09:59.716 --> 10:00.256 align:middle
from the Committee?

10:00.936 --> 10:01.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:02.326 --> 10:04.886 align:middle
So, first, we are
strongly committed

10:04.886 --> 10:07.656 align:middle
to our 2 percent inflation
objective and to achieving it

10:07.736 --> 10:10.156 align:middle
on a sustained and
symmetric basis.

10:10.946 --> 10:14.406 align:middle
As I mentioned, we think our
policy stance is appropriate

10:14.406 --> 10:16.426 align:middle
at the moment, and we
don't see a strong case

10:16.426 --> 10:17.756 align:middle
for moving in either direction.

10:18.636 --> 10:21.996 align:middle
I would point out that inflation
actually ran-including core

10:21.996 --> 10:23.676 align:middle
inflation-actually
ran pretty close

10:23.676 --> 10:26.286 align:middle
to 2 percent for much of 2018.

10:27.006 --> 10:30.636 align:middle
As you point out, both
headline and core, though,

10:31.246 --> 10:33.466 align:middle
did come in on the soft
side in the first quarter,

10:33.686 --> 10:36.846 align:middle
and that was not expected
as it relates to core.

10:37.656 --> 10:41.746 align:middle
So we say in our Statement
of Longer-Run Goals

10:41.746 --> 10:43.046 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy

10:43.046 --> 10:44.276 align:middle
that the Committee
would be concerned

10:44.276 --> 10:45.926 align:middle
if inflation were
running persistently

10:45.926 --> 10:47.296 align:middle
above or below 2 percent.

10:47.596 --> 10:49.016 align:middle
So "persistent" carries
the sense

10:49.016 --> 10:50.336 align:middle
of something that's
not transient,

10:50.856 --> 10:52.836 align:middle
something that will sustain
over a period of time.

10:53.186 --> 10:56.066 align:middle
And in this case, as we
look at these readings

10:56.066 --> 10:59.946 align:middle
in the first quarter for core,
we do see good reasons to think

10:59.946 --> 11:01.326 align:middle
that some or all

11:01.396 --> 11:05.026 align:middle
of the unexpected decrease
may wind up being transient.

11:05.706 --> 11:08.756 align:middle
And I'd point to things
like portfolio management,

11:08.756 --> 11:10.966 align:middle
service prices, apparel
prices, and other things.

11:11.176 --> 11:12.856 align:middle
In addition, the
trimmed mean measures

11:12.916 --> 11:15.076 align:middle
of inflation did
not go down as much.

11:15.076 --> 11:17.756 align:middle
Indeed, the Dallas trimmed
mean is at 2 percent.

11:18.526 --> 11:20.146 align:middle
But to go back to your question,

11:20.286 --> 11:23.486 align:middle
if we did see a
persistent-inflation running

11:23.516 --> 11:25.686 align:middle
persistently below, then that is
something the Committee would be

11:25.686 --> 11:27.956 align:middle
concerned about and
something that we would take

11:27.956 --> 11:29.366 align:middle
into account in setting policy.

11:31.206 --> 11:32.746 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

11:36.606 --> 11:37.616 align:middle
Thanks very much.

11:37.986 --> 11:40.346 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

11:40.436 --> 11:42.546 align:middle
Let me carry on on
the same theme.

11:42.546 --> 11:44.806 align:middle
There's obviously been a lot
of speculation in the markets

11:44.806 --> 11:47.866 align:middle
about the prospects for a
rate reduction this year.

11:48.386 --> 11:50.006 align:middle
Do you think markets have
effectively gotten ahead

11:50.006 --> 11:51.116 align:middle
of themselves on this?

11:51.116 --> 11:54.126 align:middle
And what sort of economic
conditions would you need to see

11:54.126 --> 11:57.086 align:middle
to give serious consideration
of a rate cut?

11:57.606 --> 11:58.746 align:middle
The discussion, for example,

11:58.746 --> 12:01.566 align:middle
about the 1995 example-do
you actually need

12:01.566 --> 12:03.696 align:middle
to see a looming
recession to cut rates,

12:03.696 --> 12:05.776 align:middle
or could an insurance
cut be appropriate?

12:06.076 --> 12:06.636 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

12:06.636 --> 12:09.636 align:middle
So as I mentioned, we've just
come through a two-day meeting,

12:09.636 --> 12:12.316 align:middle
and we've done a
deep dive on economic

12:12.316 --> 12:14.986 align:middle
and financial conditions
in the United States

12:14.986 --> 12:17.276 align:middle
and around the world and
thought about our policy,

12:17.276 --> 12:19.796 align:middle
and we do think our policy
stance is appropriate right now.

12:19.796 --> 12:23.676 align:middle
We don't see a strong case for
moving in either direction.

12:24.406 --> 12:29.516 align:middle
So- we do, of course,
though-as a routine matter,

12:29.516 --> 12:33.046 align:middle
as you well know-we look
not only at our baseline,

12:33.796 --> 12:36.606 align:middle
but we also look at alternative
simulations, both better

12:36.606 --> 12:40.816 align:middle
and worse, and we ask ourselves
what the appropriate policy

12:40.816 --> 12:41.706 align:middle
response would be.

12:41.706 --> 12:43.636 align:middle
But that's all we do.

12:43.636 --> 12:46.956 align:middle
And I would just say that we're
comfortable-the Committee is

12:46.956 --> 12:48.696 align:middle
comfortable with our
current policy stance.

12:50.066 --> 12:52.646 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

12:52.726 --> 12:54.456 align:middle
Hi. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

12:54.496 --> 12:55.396 align:middle
Shifting gears a little bit,

12:55.396 --> 12:58.096 align:middle
I wondered if you could flesh
out-I know you're describing it

12:58.096 --> 12:59.586 align:middle
as a small technical adjustment,

12:59.636 --> 13:04.936 align:middle
but on the IOER federal
funds spread, give us a sense

13:04.936 --> 13:06.876 align:middle
of why it matters whether

13:06.876 --> 13:11.516 align:middle
or not this breaches the
upper limit a little bit.

13:11.736 --> 13:14.466 align:middle
Is there any feed-through
to broader credit conditions

13:14.466 --> 13:16.666 align:middle
and financial conditions
that you worry about,

13:16.666 --> 13:19.706 align:middle
or is it simply a
matter of the Fed showing

13:19.706 --> 13:22.416 align:middle
that it can control what
it says it's controlling?

13:22.516 --> 13:24.206 align:middle
And I do have a follow-up.

13:24.336 --> 13:24.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:24.886 --> 13:30.936 align:middle
So a small, temporary
deviation outside

13:30.936 --> 13:34.466 align:middle
of the range would really
carry no-wouldn't be important,

13:34.666 --> 13:37.206 align:middle
as your question suggests.

13:37.806 --> 13:39.946 align:middle
But we do think it's
important that we be able

13:39.946 --> 13:41.946 align:middle
to control the federal
funds rate

13:41.946 --> 13:43.516 align:middle
and generally keep
it within the range.

13:43.516 --> 13:46.266 align:middle
That's just good monetary
policy, good monetary control.

13:46.346 --> 13:49.856 align:middle
So we think it's important, and
we have the tools to do that,

13:49.856 --> 13:51.196 align:middle
so we've used them again today.

13:51.196 --> 13:53.466 align:middle
And, again, this is
just a technical fix.

13:53.466 --> 13:55.586 align:middle
It really has no
implications for policy.

13:56.186 --> 13:56.476 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:56.476 --> 13:58.516 align:middle
But as a follow-up on
this, it is demonstrating

13:58.516 --> 14:01.046 align:middle
that you can control the
market you want to control.

14:01.046 --> 14:02.116 align:middle
And I guess the question is,

14:02.116 --> 14:05.546 align:middle
at what point would
these steady declines

14:05.546 --> 14:09.386 align:middle
in the IOER-steady widening of
this spread, if it continues,

14:09.856 --> 14:11.476 align:middle
essentially become
the policy choice?

14:11.476 --> 14:12.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:12.696 --> 14:14.846 align:middle
Yes, I mean-so, generally
speaking,

14:15.596 --> 14:18.986 align:middle
the federal funds rate-we
control only directly the

14:18.986 --> 14:21.506 align:middle
federal funds rate in
terms of the market rate.

14:21.506 --> 14:23.986 align:middle
And the transmission of
the federal funds rate

14:23.986 --> 14:27.366 align:middle
into other short-term
rates-money market rates-has

14:27.366 --> 14:29.316 align:middle
been very good over a
long period of time.

14:29.926 --> 14:32.356 align:middle
And that's important, because
it's really broader financial

14:32.356 --> 14:33.216 align:middle
conditions that matter,

14:33.216 --> 14:35.246 align:middle
not so much precisely
the federal funds rate.

14:35.686 --> 14:39.436 align:middle
So I think the Fed controlling
the federal funds rate is

14:39.436 --> 14:42.226 align:middle
actually important
from that standpoint,

14:42.346 --> 14:44.346 align:middle
and I don't see us
not controlling it.

14:44.346 --> 14:48.456 align:middle
So I think we'll continue to
control it, and it will continue

14:48.456 --> 14:50.436 align:middle
to transmit well into
broader financial conditions.

14:50.926 --> 14:56.156 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

14:56.156 --> 14:57.696 align:middle
Michael McKee with Bloomberg.

14:58.126 --> 15:02.426 align:middle
I'm curious about the
financial conditions

15:02.506 --> 15:03.896 align:middle
that you see out there.

15:03.896 --> 15:05.556 align:middle
The minutes of the
March meeting tell us

15:05.916 --> 15:09.226 align:middle
"a few" officials worried about
financial stability risks.

15:09.546 --> 15:11.946 align:middle
Was there a broader
discussion at this meeting?

15:12.056 --> 15:14.896 align:middle
Any consensus on whether
such risks are growing

15:14.896 --> 15:18.976 align:middle
as the markets hit new highs,
and we do see some instability

15:18.976 --> 15:21.436 align:middle
in short-end trading-is
it possible

15:21.656 --> 15:23.496 align:middle
that rates are too
low at this point?

15:24.186 --> 15:24.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:25.056 --> 15:28.416 align:middle
We actually have a
financial stability briefing

15:28.416 --> 15:32.026 align:middle
and opportunity for
comment every other meeting.

15:32.026 --> 15:36.186 align:middle
So we had our quarterly
briefing today-yesterday,

15:36.186 --> 15:38.426 align:middle
as a matter of fact-and had
that discussion as well.

15:39.166 --> 15:40.916 align:middle
And I think there haven't
been a lot of changes

15:40.916 --> 15:42.986 align:middle
since the last meeting,
but I'll just go

15:43.036 --> 15:43.906 align:middle
through the way we
think about it.

15:43.906 --> 15:46.096 align:middle
First, we've developed and
published our framework

15:46.096 --> 15:48.956 align:middle
for assessing financial
stability vulnerabilities-put it

15:48.956 --> 15:51.676 align:middle
out for comment and
welcome any feedback

15:51.676 --> 15:53.046 align:middle
that we get from the public.

15:53.316 --> 15:57.246 align:middle
And that enables us to focus
our assessments regularly

15:57.246 --> 16:00.016 align:middle
on the same things so that
we can be held accountable

16:00.016 --> 16:00.876 align:middle
and be transparent.

16:01.246 --> 16:04.606 align:middle
So there are four aspects of it
that I'll go through quickly,

16:04.606 --> 16:07.146 align:middle
but I'd say that the
headline really is

16:07.186 --> 16:09.626 align:middle
that while there
are some concerns

16:09.626 --> 16:12.746 align:middle
around nonfinancial
corporate debt,

16:13.976 --> 16:17.116 align:middle
really the finding
is that, overall,

16:17.416 --> 16:21.596 align:middle
financial stability
vulnerabilities are moderate,

16:21.596 --> 16:23.036 align:middle
on balance.

16:23.146 --> 16:24.496 align:middle
And, in addition, I would say

16:24.496 --> 16:29.276 align:middle
that the financial system
is quite resilient to shocks

16:29.276 --> 16:31.476 align:middle
of various kinds with high
capital and liquidity.

16:31.776 --> 16:34.286 align:middle
But the four things we look
at are, first, asset prices.

16:34.286 --> 16:35.796 align:middle
And as for asset prices,

16:36.476 --> 16:38.376 align:middle
some asset prices are
somewhat elevated,

16:38.376 --> 16:40.006 align:middle
but I would say not
extremely so.

16:40.476 --> 16:43.606 align:middle
Leverage in the financial
sector-I mentioned households

16:43.606 --> 16:46.356 align:middle
are actually in good shape
from a leverage standpoint.

16:46.356 --> 16:47.306 align:middle
Default rates are low.

16:47.306 --> 16:48.586 align:middle
Borrowing is relatively low.

16:49.086 --> 16:52.866 align:middle
Nonfinancial corporates is an
area that we've spotlighted

16:53.016 --> 16:56.696 align:middle
and focused on for attention,
and there are concerns

16:56.696 --> 16:59.416 align:middle
about that-not so much from a
financial stability standpoint,

16:59.516 --> 17:00.426 align:middle
but from the standpoint

17:00.426 --> 17:03.026 align:middle
that having a highly levered
corporate sector could be an

17:03.026 --> 17:05.636 align:middle
amplifier for a downturn.

17:06.226 --> 17:08.936 align:middle
And then the last two-the
last two things are really

17:08.936 --> 17:11.676 align:middle
about the leverage in
the financial system

17:11.746 --> 17:13.656 align:middle
and funding risk, and
those are both very,

17:13.656 --> 17:15.726 align:middle
very low by historic
standards in the United States.

17:15.726 --> 17:19.246 align:middle
So, on balance, in my view,
vulnerabilities are moderate.

17:19.526 --> 17:19.846 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

17:19.846 --> 17:21.756 align:middle
If I could follow up on
that-I'm just curious

17:21.756 --> 17:26.016 align:middle
as to whether the level of asset
prices is a reason why you might

17:26.126 --> 17:31.336 align:middle
not be interested
in cutting rates.

17:31.336 --> 17:32.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:32.696 --> 17:35.546 align:middle
So we do say that risks to
the financial system-we say

17:35.546 --> 17:38.956 align:middle
in our longer-run
statement of goals

17:38.956 --> 17:42.176 align:middle
and monetary policy
strategy that risks

17:42.176 --> 17:43.986 align:middle
to the financial system
that could prevent us

17:44.046 --> 17:45.516 align:middle
from achieving our
goals are something

17:45.516 --> 17:47.496 align:middle
that we do take into
consideration.

17:48.276 --> 17:50.566 align:middle
I would say that, though,
that really the tools

17:50.566 --> 17:55.926 align:middle
for addressing those concerns
are better-capital liquidity,

17:56.196 --> 17:59.326 align:middle
good supervision, good stress
testing, and things like that.

17:59.476 --> 18:02.496 align:middle
Those are better first-order
tools to deal with these kinds

18:02.496 --> 18:03.706 align:middle
of issues than monetary policy.

18:05.196 --> 18:06.206 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:08.286 --> 18:12.316 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

18:12.726 --> 18:14.816 align:middle
Chair Powell, with the
benefit of hindsight,

18:15.266 --> 18:18.466 align:middle
did last year's rate increases
make it harder for the Fed

18:18.466 --> 18:23.076 align:middle
to credibly affirm its 2 percent
symmetric inflation target is

18:23.076 --> 18:24.246 align:middle
not, in fact, a ceiling?

18:24.766 --> 18:27.446 align:middle
And, if so, would it be
appropriate to lower rates

18:27.636 --> 18:30.466 align:middle
if core inflation remained
persistently closer

18:30.466 --> 18:32.376 align:middle
to 1½ percent instead
of 2 percent?

18:32.876 --> 18:36.096 align:middle
And, if not, do you worry
about any unwelcome tightening

18:36.096 --> 18:39.316 align:middle
in real rates, given the recent
softness in core inflation?

18:40.566 --> 18:41.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:41.486 --> 18:45.556 align:middle
So, to your first question,
if you go back and think

18:45.556 --> 18:48.976 align:middle
about the middle of last year,
inflation was at 2 percent

18:49.556 --> 18:52.886 align:middle
and appeared to be
staying there.

18:53.316 --> 18:56.226 align:middle
And the economy was quite
strong and was growing strong.

18:56.226 --> 18:58.786 align:middle
The fiscal changes were
hitting the economy

18:58.786 --> 19:00.386 align:middle
in a very positive way.

19:00.846 --> 19:05.176 align:middle
And so I think the
expectation was

19:05.176 --> 19:07.176 align:middle
that inflation would
remain up around 2 percent.

19:07.606 --> 19:11.736 align:middle
The weak first quarter
performance was not expected-of

19:11.736 --> 19:13.916 align:middle
core was not expected, and
I don't think is related

19:13.916 --> 19:16.926 align:middle
to anything we did, in
terms of raising rates.

19:16.996 --> 19:20.176 align:middle
It appears to be
more-we don't know this,

19:20.236 --> 19:22.836 align:middle
but you never know
until-with hindsight,

19:22.836 --> 19:25.416 align:middle
with perfect hindsight,
but some of it does appear

19:25.416 --> 19:27.146 align:middle
to be transient or
idiosyncratic.

19:27.146 --> 19:28.456 align:middle
Now, the second part
of your question?

19:28.456 --> 19:30.706 align:middle
Sorry. NICK TIMIRAOS.

19:31.096 --> 19:38.116 align:middle
Well, I mean, if it was an
issue, would it be appropriate

19:38.256 --> 19:41.986 align:middle
to lower rates if core inflation
held closer to 1½ percent?

19:42.516 --> 19:43.796 align:middle
And, if not, are you worried

19:44.236 --> 19:45.936 align:middle
that there is unwanted
tightening

19:46.066 --> 19:48.296 align:middle
from real rates,
being where they are?

19:48.336 --> 19:48.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:48.986 --> 19:53.236 align:middle
Yes, I mean-so, as I said
earlier, we do address this

19:53.236 --> 19:55.906 align:middle
in our, sort of,
constitutional document.

19:57.006 --> 20:00.776 align:middle
If inflation were to run
persistently below 2 percent

20:00.776 --> 20:03.136 align:middle
or persistently above 2
percent, that would be a concern

20:03.136 --> 20:05.216 align:middle
for the Committee, and
the Committee would take

20:05.216 --> 20:07.246 align:middle
that into account
in making policy.

20:07.836 --> 20:12.366 align:middle
I do think it's important
that inflation run close to

20:12.366 --> 20:14.316 align:middle
and sustainably -for
a sustained period

20:14.316 --> 20:16.446 align:middle
of time-and symmetrically
around 2 percent.

20:16.496 --> 20:19.026 align:middle
Because if it doesn't,
you run the risk

20:19.026 --> 20:23.036 align:middle
that inflation expectations
can-it has been the case,

20:24.006 --> 20:27.336 align:middle
most of the misses are on the
downside-inflation expectations

20:27.336 --> 20:28.816 align:middle
over time could be pulled down,

20:28.816 --> 20:32.246 align:middle
and that could put downward
pressure on inflation

20:32.246 --> 20:35.586 align:middle
and make it harder for us to
react to downturns and harder

20:35.586 --> 20:39.176 align:middle
for us to, you know, support
the economy in difficult times.

20:40.456 --> 20:41.386 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

20:44.836 --> 20:46.896 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, New York Times.

20:47.206 --> 20:50.556 align:middle
So as you just mentioned, last
year when you guys were kind

20:50.796 --> 20:52.936 align:middle
of getting inflation
coming in around 2 percent,

20:53.156 --> 20:56.236 align:middle
you had this benefit of the
tailwind of fiscal stimulus.

20:56.236 --> 20:58.446 align:middle
And you still have
that to some extent,

20:58.656 --> 21:00.666 align:middle
although the tax cuts have
mostly feeded through-we've

21:00.826 --> 21:02.536 align:middle
still got something
in the pipeline

21:02.536 --> 21:03.766 align:middle
from the spending cap increases.

21:03.766 --> 21:06.826 align:middle
So, I guess, how do you
think about inflation

21:06.896 --> 21:12.636 align:middle
as that fiscal benefit wanes
toward the end of this year?

21:13.336 --> 21:13.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:13.826 --> 21:15.516 align:middle
Inflation, first of
all-month-to-month,

21:15.516 --> 21:16.826 align:middle
quarter-to-quarter-is
going to move around.

21:16.826 --> 21:18.726 align:middle
There will always be
factors hitting it.

21:18.726 --> 21:23.736 align:middle
So probably the biggest single
factor driving it is the rate

21:23.736 --> 21:26.716 align:middle
of underlying inflation or
the closely related idea

21:26.716 --> 21:30.116 align:middle
of where inflation expectations
are anchored-the thought being,

21:30.116 --> 21:33.096 align:middle
that's where inflation
will go in the long run

21:33.096 --> 21:35.976 align:middle
if it's not been pushed
by those other factors.

21:36.866 --> 21:40.536 align:middle
So we also think that
slack-the level of slack

21:40.536 --> 21:42.776 align:middle
in the economy does
play some small role.

21:42.776 --> 21:45.716 align:middle
It's actually still
a measurable role.

21:45.716 --> 21:47.516 align:middle
It's nothing like
it was in the 1960s

21:47.516 --> 21:48.966 align:middle
when the Phillips
curve was quite steep,

21:49.606 --> 21:53.366 align:middle
so that's also something
that plays a role.

21:54.166 --> 21:59.666 align:middle
And so we take all those things
into account, and the part of it

21:59.666 --> 22:02.596 align:middle
that we can control
is the slack part.

22:03.236 --> 22:07.536 align:middle
And, again, we do expect that
this reading will be transient

22:07.536 --> 22:08.776 align:middle
and inflation will move back up.

22:08.776 --> 22:13.986 align:middle
And if it isn't and if it runs
persistently below 2 percent

22:14.106 --> 22:16.666 align:middle
for a sustained period, then
that's something we'd take

22:16.666 --> 22:20.246 align:middle
into account in setting policy.

22:22.066 --> 22:24.166 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

22:24.166 --> 22:26.586 align:middle
Chairman Powell,
Donna Borak with CNN.

22:26.626 --> 22:29.946 align:middle
Pivoting a little
bit-about wages, since 2010,

22:30.036 --> 22:33.866 align:middle
women's real earnings have gone
up about 3.9 percent compared

22:33.866 --> 22:36.846 align:middle
to men's, which have
risen about 2.1.

22:37.506 --> 22:39.296 align:middle
Do you think the
relative increase

22:39.336 --> 22:42.156 align:middle
in women's wages is a
problem for the U.S. economy?

22:42.386 --> 22:44.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:45.266 --> 22:47.776 align:middle
You know, I think, generally,

22:47.896 --> 22:51.256 align:middle
I'd have to see the
data on that.

22:51.256 --> 22:53.926 align:middle
It sounds like you picked
a particular time frame.

22:53.926 --> 22:56.316 align:middle
Over time, I really wonder
whether that's the case.

22:56.916 --> 23:00.126 align:middle
You know, I think men and
women should make the same

23:00.126 --> 23:03.366 align:middle
for the same work, by and large.

23:04.076 --> 23:08.166 align:middle
So- DONNA BORAK.

23:11.136 --> 23:12.796 align:middle
Just to push a little
bit on this.

23:12.926 --> 23:16.756 align:middle
But if the data shows that
women's wages are rising higher,

23:16.756 --> 23:19.436 align:middle
is there a damage
to the U.S. economy

23:19.436 --> 23:22.866 align:middle
if male's wages are declining or
not growing as fast as women's?

23:23.196 --> 23:23.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:23.456 --> 23:27.136 align:middle
So I think we're getting in
here to commenting on a nominee

23:27.136 --> 23:28.306 align:middle
to the Fed indirectly,

23:28.336 --> 23:30.286 align:middle
and that's something
I'd rather avoid.

23:30.286 --> 23:34.436 align:middle
It's really not my role to
engage with potential nominees

23:34.496 --> 23:37.126 align:middle
to the Fed, so I'm really
not going to go there.

23:37.126 --> 23:38.406 align:middle
I haven't seen this
research either,

23:38.406 --> 23:39.716 align:middle
so I don't really-really know.

23:40.156 --> 23:46.246 align:middle
Thanks. VICTORIA GUIDA.

23:47.186 --> 23:48.736 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

23:49.026 --> 23:53.016 align:middle
I wanted to ask-early last
month, I believe it was April 2,

23:53.156 --> 23:55.086 align:middle
the Fedwire® system went down.

23:55.516 --> 23:58.516 align:middle
And I was just wondering
if you could talk

23:58.516 --> 24:01.626 align:middle
about what happened
there, how long it lasted,

24:02.026 --> 24:04.586 align:middle
whether you know what happened,
whether you're still looking

24:04.586 --> 24:07.176 align:middle
into it, and whether
it's something

24:07.176 --> 24:08.626 align:middle
that could happen again.

24:08.626 --> 24:09.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:09.436 --> 24:10.516 align:middle
Sure. So that's right.

24:10.516 --> 24:13.866 align:middle
I want to say it was April 1,
but it may have been April 2.

24:13.866 --> 24:18.206 align:middle
In any case, the Fedwire®
did go down for a few hours,

24:18.206 --> 24:19.956 align:middle
in the three- or
four-hour range.

24:20.526 --> 24:23.076 align:middle
We were able to quickly
identify the problem:

24:23.076 --> 24:26.856 align:middle
It was an internal problem,
and we were able to correct it

24:26.856 --> 24:30.076 align:middle
and make changes so that that
problem and other problems

24:30.076 --> 24:32.436 align:middle
like it cannot repeat
themselves.

24:32.436 --> 24:35.096 align:middle
So, you know, we learn
from these instances.

24:35.096 --> 24:37.766 align:middle
They're fairly rare,
but we learn from them,

24:37.766 --> 24:40.406 align:middle
and in this case
it was internal,

24:40.406 --> 24:41.256 align:middle
and it's been corrected.

24:42.996 --> 24:44.926 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

24:46.816 --> 24:49.336 align:middle
Steve Matthews with Bloomberg.

24:49.336 --> 24:51.786 align:middle
Next month, we have the
10th anniversary of the end

24:51.786 --> 24:54.526 align:middle
of the recession, and
there are some countries

24:54.526 --> 24:58.466 align:middle
that have had expansions
for 15, 20, 25 years.

24:58.896 --> 25:02.166 align:middle
Do you think that's something
that's practical for the U.S.,

25:02.166 --> 25:05.346 align:middle
that we could have that
kind of lengthy expansion?

25:05.806 --> 25:07.976 align:middle
And for you personally,

25:07.976 --> 25:10.516 align:middle
if we had a recession
during your tenure,

25:10.796 --> 25:12.116 align:middle
would you consider
that a failure?

25:12.616 --> 25:14.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:15.106 --> 25:17.376 align:middle
You know, I wouldn't
want to speculate.

25:17.376 --> 25:20.586 align:middle
There's always the example
of Australia that everyone,

25:20.586 --> 25:21.876 align:middle
I think, is aware of,
where I think they're

25:21.876 --> 25:24.306 align:middle
in year 28 of their expansion.

25:24.906 --> 25:29.096 align:middle
So things are possible.

25:29.096 --> 25:31.076 align:middle
All I can see is that
we have an economy

25:31.076 --> 25:34.206 align:middle
where the expansion
is continuing.

25:34.206 --> 25:35.986 align:middle
Growth is at a healthy level.

25:36.276 --> 25:37.286 align:middle
The labor market is strong.

25:37.286 --> 25:38.356 align:middle
We see job creation.

25:38.356 --> 25:39.466 align:middle
We see wages moving up.

25:40.186 --> 25:42.876 align:middle
Inflation is low, which gives
us the ability to be patient,

25:43.516 --> 25:46.346 align:middle
and we do expect it to
move up, and we want it

25:46.346 --> 25:47.496 align:middle
to move up to 2 percent.

25:47.496 --> 25:51.946 align:middle
So I see us on a good
path for this year.

25:52.526 --> 25:53.146 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

25:53.146 --> 25:58.736 align:middle
Do you see parallels with
the 1990s, when-for example,

25:58.736 --> 26:01.156 align:middle
some people have
pointed out, in the U.S.,

26:01.256 --> 26:04.946 align:middle
the longest expansion before
this one, there was a rate cut

26:04.946 --> 26:08.326 align:middle
in 1995, and rates went up,
and then they came down,

26:08.326 --> 26:10.196 align:middle
and there was that
kind of management.

26:10.196 --> 26:13.996 align:middle
Do you see similarities
in today's situation?

26:13.996 --> 26:14.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:14.206 --> 26:15.596 align:middle
Similarities in the length.

26:15.596 --> 26:18.066 align:middle
I mean, the situations
were quite different then.

26:18.066 --> 26:21.216 align:middle
This was before inflation
really was under control, but,

26:21.216 --> 26:25.256 align:middle
you know, it's very interesting
to look at the history.

26:25.256 --> 26:28.656 align:middle
I find it quite interesting
to look at different periods.

26:29.256 --> 26:33.626 align:middle
But I think, in our own cycle,
we face a particular set

26:33.626 --> 26:36.716 align:middle
of challenges that are really
what's relevant for us now.

26:37.116 --> 26:38.386 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

26:38.806 --> 26:44.676 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Marty
Crutsinger with the AP.

26:44.676 --> 26:46.596 align:middle
You've repeatedly said
that the Fed's going

26:46.596 --> 26:48.946 align:middle
to conduct monetary
policy without regard

26:48.996 --> 26:51.796 align:middle
to outside political
pressure, but it seems

26:51.796 --> 26:53.866 align:middle
like the President is
intent on increasing

26:53.866 --> 26:54.896 align:middle
that political pressure.

26:55.246 --> 26:58.526 align:middle
Yesterday, he said you should
cut rates a full percentage

26:58.526 --> 27:00.566 align:middle
point and start quantitative
easing.

27:01.746 --> 27:03.346 align:middle
What do those comments do,

27:03.346 --> 27:08.166 align:middle
in terms of affecting
how you pursue policy

27:08.756 --> 27:11.966 align:middle
and how you convey your
decisions to the public?

27:13.076 --> 27:13.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:14.106 --> 27:18.206 align:middle
Yes, so, as you know, we are
a nonpolitical institution,

27:18.446 --> 27:19.696 align:middle
and that means we don't think

27:19.696 --> 27:21.536 align:middle
about short-term
political considerations,

27:21.536 --> 27:23.906 align:middle
we don't discuss them,
and we don't consider them

27:23.906 --> 27:26.116 align:middle
in making our decisions
one way or the other.

27:26.976 --> 27:30.596 align:middle
And what we're always solving
for in our process, in our work,

27:30.596 --> 27:31.906 align:middle
is to carry out our mission,

27:32.196 --> 27:34.076 align:middle
which is to extend the
economic expansion,

27:34.076 --> 27:35.376 align:middle
keep the labor market strong,

27:35.556 --> 27:37.076 align:middle
and get inflation
around 2 percent.

27:37.526 --> 27:40.316 align:middle
So to give you an idea of
what our process is like,

27:40.316 --> 27:42.946 align:middle
maybe as a way of putting
all that in context-so

27:43.006 --> 27:45.266 align:middle
for the past maybe 10 days,

27:45.336 --> 27:50.686 align:middle
all 17 FOMC members will have
made extensive preparations:

27:50.686 --> 27:53.426 align:middle
catching up on the latest
data, reading all the memos,

27:53.756 --> 27:55.736 align:middle
talking to their
colleagues and their staff.

27:55.926 --> 27:58.336 align:middle
As you also are I'm
sure aware, Marty,

27:58.946 --> 28:02.446 align:middle
we talk to literally
thousands of businesspeople

28:02.446 --> 28:05.076 align:middle
and market people and people
in the nonprofit sector

28:05.076 --> 28:07.196 align:middle
and the educational sector just

28:07.196 --> 28:08.396 align:middle
to get a better sense
of the economy.

28:08.396 --> 28:12.386 align:middle
We put all that together, and
we come together for two days.

28:12.876 --> 28:15.936 align:middle
The first day begins with an
economic briefing, which is,

28:15.936 --> 28:18.026 align:middle
sort of, economic and
financial developments

28:18.026 --> 28:19.436 align:middle
in the United States
and around the world.

28:19.726 --> 28:22.296 align:middle
That takes up most
of the first day,

28:22.296 --> 28:24.066 align:middle
and we talk about
this in great detail.

28:24.616 --> 28:26.426 align:middle
We go away, we think about
that, and we come back,

28:26.426 --> 28:28.566 align:middle
and the next day we talk
about monetary policy.

28:29.036 --> 28:35.516 align:middle
And in this particular case, we
came to a unanimous decision,

28:35.516 --> 28:36.916 align:middle
after an extensive discussion,

28:36.986 --> 28:39.976 align:middle
that our monetary policy stance
is appropriate where it is.

28:40.476 --> 28:43.026 align:middle
And we think our monetary policy
stance is in a good place,

28:43.026 --> 28:45.136 align:middle
and we're going to be patient
as we consider adjustments.

28:45.656 --> 28:48.486 align:middle
And we also see, by the way,
the evolving-risk picture

28:48.486 --> 28:51.396 align:middle
as very consistent
with that outlook.

28:51.846 --> 28:54.596 align:middle
So we don't feel like the data's
pushing us in either direction.

28:55.296 --> 28:58.416 align:middle
Of course, we'll not
hesitate if we do feel

28:58.416 --> 29:00.436 align:middle
that the data justify
moving in either direction.

29:00.506 --> 29:01.776 align:middle
But that's our process.

29:01.776 --> 29:02.926 align:middle
That's how we think
about things.

29:03.056 --> 29:04.636 align:middle
We don't think about
other factors.

29:05.326 --> 29:06.936 align:middle
We don't let them into
our decisionmaking.

29:06.936 --> 29:08.316 align:middle
We don't discuss them.

29:09.806 --> 29:10.496 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

29:11.846 --> 29:13.626 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

29:13.626 --> 29:15.046 align:middle
Thanks for the question.

29:15.046 --> 29:19.146 align:middle
In the last 23 years,
core PCE inflation has run

29:19.146 --> 29:22.086 align:middle
above 2 percent only during
the period that coincided

29:22.086 --> 29:23.056 align:middle
with the housing bubble.

29:23.556 --> 29:26.736 align:middle
I'd like to know what you
would say to people who worry

29:26.736 --> 29:30.106 align:middle
that it will prove difficult
for the Fed to lift inflation

29:30.666 --> 29:33.946 align:middle
without potentially
stoking another asset bubble

29:33.946 --> 29:34.546 align:middle
of some sort.

29:34.886 --> 29:35.156 align:middle
Thank you.

29:35.656 --> 29:36.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:36.836 --> 29:40.336 align:middle
Yes. So you're pointing
to, really,

29:40.456 --> 29:45.286 align:middle
the fact that in recent years
inflation has moved down

29:45.286 --> 29:47.426 align:middle
and down, and, really,

29:47.836 --> 29:50.756 align:middle
many major central
banks have struggled

29:50.756 --> 29:53.526 align:middle
to reach their inflation
goals from below.

29:54.206 --> 29:57.146 align:middle
And that includes us, although
we've actually done-we've come

29:57.146 --> 29:59.116 align:middle
closer, I think,
than most others.

29:59.646 --> 30:04.336 align:middle
And it's just a question,
I think, of demographic

30:04.336 --> 30:08.166 align:middle
and other large and, in
some cases, global forces

30:08.906 --> 30:12.756 align:middle
that are disinflationary
to some extent,

30:13.576 --> 30:15.886 align:middle
and it creates significant
challenges.

30:15.886 --> 30:17.496 align:middle
One, I would say, is, it means

30:17.496 --> 30:19.656 align:middle
that interest rates will
be lower-will be closer

30:19.656 --> 30:21.726 align:middle
to the effective lower
bound more of the time

30:22.046 --> 30:23.476 align:middle
because that means
lower interest rates.

30:23.476 --> 30:26.926 align:middle
And that's one of the
reasons we're having a review

30:26.926 --> 30:29.716 align:middle
of our monetary policy
strategies, tools,

30:29.716 --> 30:33.716 align:middle
and communications this year-to
think about that problem.

30:33.766 --> 30:36.286 align:middle
You mentioned the connection
to financial stability

30:36.286 --> 30:39.236 align:middle
of lower-for-longer rates,
and that is another challenge.

30:39.836 --> 30:42.536 align:middle
As I mentioned earlier,

30:42.536 --> 30:45.446 align:middle
we do consider financial
stability concerns

30:45.446 --> 30:48.616 align:middle
to the extent they threaten
achievement of our goals,

30:48.656 --> 30:56.106 align:middle
but we also view, and I do take
the view that macroprudential

30:56.106 --> 30:59.086 align:middle
and supervisory tools,
regulatory tools,

30:59.586 --> 31:01.906 align:middle
things like the stress
tests that we can do right

31:01.906 --> 31:04.026 align:middle
through the cycle-those
are really the best defense

31:04.026 --> 31:05.306 align:middle
against financial instability

31:05.306 --> 31:09.336 align:middle
so that the financial system is
highly resilient to the kinds

31:09.336 --> 31:11.046 align:middle
of financial shocks
that can happen.

31:13.976 --> 31:19.266 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

31:20.056 --> 31:20.616 align:middle
Thank you.

31:21.026 --> 31:22.426 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

31:23.376 --> 31:27.346 align:middle
This morning, the ISM
manufacturing index fell

31:27.396 --> 31:31.026 align:middle
to have its worst reading
since October 2016.

31:32.016 --> 31:35.846 align:middle
So isn't that a dark cloud on
your outlook for strong growth

31:36.026 --> 31:36.896 align:middle
for the rest of the year?

31:36.896 --> 31:41.816 align:middle
I mean, how much weight does it
hold for you, and is it a sign

31:41.816 --> 31:43.376 align:middle
that monetary policy
might be too tight?

31:44.026 --> 31:44.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:44.466 --> 31:46.976 align:middle
This is the ISM reading, I guess

31:46.976 --> 31:48.816 align:middle
from this morning,
on manufacturing.

31:49.116 --> 31:53.266 align:middle
Yes, we see that reading
as-it's still a positive reading

31:53.266 --> 31:54.816 align:middle
and consistent with
what we expect

31:54.816 --> 31:57.016 align:middle
from the manufacturing
sector, which is moderate

31:57.016 --> 31:58.706 align:middle
or perhaps modest growth.

31:59.526 --> 32:02.646 align:middle
Manufacturing has been
weak all around the world.

32:02.646 --> 32:04.386 align:middle
Services have been
growing faster.

32:05.006 --> 32:09.916 align:middle
So it's-yes, it's something
that we are watching carefully,

32:09.966 --> 32:12.276 align:middle
but we do expect a positive
contribution to growth

32:12.276 --> 32:13.266 align:middle
from the manufacturing sector.

32:15.056 --> 32:16.676 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:20.486 --> 32:21.826 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

32:21.826 --> 32:23.786 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for doing this.

32:23.956 --> 32:24.516 align:middle
You mentioned-talked

32:24.516 --> 32:26.926 align:middle
about domestic growth a
little bit with the economy.

32:26.926 --> 32:30.116 align:middle
You mentioned that the progress
in the talks with China trade,

32:30.396 --> 32:33.646 align:middle
progress in talks with Japanese
trade, the USMCA is moving

32:33.646 --> 32:34.986 align:middle
or going to move
through Congress.

32:35.246 --> 32:37.446 align:middle
Could you talk about a little
bit of the domestic growth

32:37.446 --> 32:39.496 align:middle
that you're seeing going forward
for the rest of the year?

32:39.496 --> 32:43.926 align:middle
And could these trade deals turn
into tailwinds for the economy?

32:45.076 --> 32:45.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:45.996 --> 32:49.906 align:middle
Our outlook and my outlook
is for-is a positive one,

32:49.906 --> 32:52.826 align:middle
is a healthy one for the
U.S. economy for growth

32:52.826 --> 32:53.826 align:middle
for the rest of this year.

32:54.426 --> 32:57.746 align:middle
And I would say that the
basis for that, really,

32:57.746 --> 33:01.006 align:middle
is consumer spending
and business investment.

33:01.006 --> 33:03.646 align:middle
So if you look at
consumer spending,

33:03.776 --> 33:07.496 align:middle
you saw stronger retail sales,

33:07.496 --> 33:09.516 align:middle
stronger motor vehicle
sales in March.

33:10.206 --> 33:12.506 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
the conditions,

33:12.506 --> 33:14.786 align:middle
the broader economic
fundamentals,

33:14.786 --> 33:16.416 align:middle
are strong in support

33:16.416 --> 33:19.386 align:middle
of consumer spending-that's
more accommodative financial

33:19.386 --> 33:21.236 align:middle
conditions, high
confidence readings,

33:21.786 --> 33:23.886 align:middle
high levels of employment,
wages going up.

33:23.886 --> 33:25.866 align:middle
All those things are going
to support consumer spending.

33:25.866 --> 33:31.396 align:middle
So that's a significant part
of the outlook this year.

33:31.396 --> 33:35.606 align:middle
And business investment
should also be positive

33:35.666 --> 33:37.496 align:middle
in that sort of direction.

33:38.046 --> 33:40.516 align:middle
In terms of the effect
of trade deals,

33:40.516 --> 33:46.096 align:middle
I think one thing would
be that the resolution

33:46.096 --> 33:49.056 align:middle
of the uncertainty around
these trade negotiations would,

33:49.486 --> 33:51.786 align:middle
I would guess, be a positive
for business sentiment.

33:52.626 --> 33:55.236 align:middle
We have been hearing from our
business contacts, really,

33:55.236 --> 33:57.616 align:middle
a lot since the beginning
of the trade negotiations

33:57.616 --> 34:00.066 align:middle
that uncertainty is a concern.

34:00.066 --> 34:03.356 align:middle
If you import metals or
whatever for your product

34:03.676 --> 34:07.096 align:middle
or export your product, then
it's been a challenge for you.

34:07.756 --> 34:09.806 align:middle
So that would be a positive.

34:09.806 --> 34:13.456 align:middle
And, of course, most of the
gains, though, I would expect,

34:13.456 --> 34:16.366 align:middle
even from a successful
trade negotiation,

34:16.666 --> 34:18.036 align:middle
would come in over time.

34:18.236 --> 34:20.066 align:middle
You know, I don't think-it
wouldn't be the kind of thing

34:20.066 --> 34:23.306 align:middle
where you'd immediately
feel big effects right away,

34:23.306 --> 34:25.886 align:middle
but they could be quite
important over a longer period.

34:26.416 --> 34:29.756 align:middle
So that, I think-that
would be my expectation.

34:29.966 --> 34:31.336 align:middle
I don't know, though.

34:31.336 --> 34:34.416 align:middle
I haven't seen-none of us,
at least I haven't seen,

34:34.506 --> 34:36.436 align:middle
the details of what's
been negotiated.

34:36.946 --> 34:42.996 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

34:44.036 --> 34:46.896 align:middle
Getting back to inflation,
can you talk more

34:46.896 --> 34:50.956 align:middle
about the transitory factors
holding down inflation,

34:50.956 --> 34:52.536 align:middle
how significant they are,

34:52.826 --> 34:56.296 align:middle
and why you think those
factors will pass?

34:56.596 --> 34:57.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:57.456 --> 34:59.496 align:middle
Sure. Well, I would
just mention a couple.

34:59.496 --> 35:01.996 align:middle
Let me say, I don't mean
to diminish concerns

35:01.996 --> 35:07.036 align:middle
about too-low inflation, but
I think there's good reason

35:07.036 --> 35:10.156 align:middle
to think that these readings
are particularly influenced

35:10.156 --> 35:11.506 align:middle
by some transitory factors.

35:11.506 --> 35:15.376 align:middle
One that I would mention is
portfolio management services,

35:15.796 --> 35:18.456 align:middle
which would tend to go down
when asset prices go down,

35:18.536 --> 35:19.126 align:middle
with a lag.

35:19.616 --> 35:21.556 align:middle
And so when asset
prices went back up,

35:21.556 --> 35:24.596 align:middle
probably there'll be
a swing around there,

35:24.596 --> 35:25.766 align:middle
a positive contribution.

35:26.266 --> 35:29.676 align:middle
Other ones that get mentioned
are things like apparel,

35:29.676 --> 35:31.966 align:middle
and apparel prices
were very, very low.

35:31.966 --> 35:33.426 align:middle
There was a change
in the methodology.

35:34.296 --> 35:36.586 align:middle
And another one is airfares.

35:36.586 --> 35:37.866 align:middle
There are many little things.

35:38.236 --> 35:43.316 align:middle
So we don't know until, again,
until we see, but there's reason

35:43.316 --> 35:45.846 align:middle
to think that those would be
transient and would turn around.

35:46.106 --> 35:49.186 align:middle
And another way to look at it
is, there are models that look

35:49.186 --> 35:54.446 align:middle
at inflation in different ways,
like-not models, but measures,

35:54.446 --> 35:56.746 align:middle
like the Dallas trimmed
mean, as I mentioned.

35:56.746 --> 36:02.476 align:middle
So trimmed mean-it cuts off
the big movements on the upside

36:02.476 --> 36:04.576 align:middle
and the downside and looks
at just the mean movements

36:04.576 --> 36:06.456 align:middle
in inflation of various
product categories

36:06.456 --> 36:08.856 align:middle
and service categories, and
it didn't go down at all.

36:08.856 --> 36:11.116 align:middle
It's at 2 percent-or it's at, I
don't know whether it went down

36:11.116 --> 36:12.786 align:middle
or was above that, but
it's at 2 percent now.

36:12.786 --> 36:15.816 align:middle
So there's reason to think
that these will be transient.

36:15.816 --> 36:17.756 align:middle
We, of course, will be
watching very carefully to see

36:17.756 --> 36:18.626 align:middle
that that is the case.

36:18.956 --> 36:22.516 align:middle
I would point to the case
of cell phone services.

36:22.516 --> 36:25.096 align:middle
Many of you will
remember, in March of 2017,

36:25.446 --> 36:27.086 align:middle
there was a very low reading

36:27.456 --> 36:32.786 align:middle
for cell phone services-mobile
phone services-and it was kind

36:32.786 --> 36:36.846 align:middle
of a price war, and it
dragged down core inflation

36:36.846 --> 36:39.666 align:middle
for a full year, but it
did not look like something

36:39.666 --> 36:40.876 align:middle
that would be repeated.

36:41.476 --> 36:44.646 align:middle
And we kind of thought so and
said so, and then, sure enough,

36:44.646 --> 36:49.836 align:middle
in 2018, we had those months
of 2 percent inflation.

36:50.526 --> 36:52.926 align:middle
So, again, we'll
have to see here.

36:52.926 --> 36:54.416 align:middle
We're going to be
watching-really,

36:54.416 --> 36:56.986 align:middle
I'd like to say we're going to
be watching inflation carefully

36:56.986 --> 37:00.286 align:middle
to see that these things
are transient, and I'll end

37:00.286 --> 37:03.696 align:middle
by saying we are
strongly committed

37:03.696 --> 37:05.236 align:middle
to the 2 percent
inflation objective.

37:07.026 --> 37:07.766 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

37:08.946 --> 37:11.056 align:middle
Just following up, Chair Powell.

37:11.166 --> 37:13.766 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

37:13.766 --> 37:17.226 align:middle
You were saying if
inflation does stay low

37:17.226 --> 37:19.586 align:middle
and these low inflation
rates are not transient,

37:19.586 --> 37:21.966 align:middle
you said a couple
times you'll take

37:21.966 --> 37:24.056 align:middle
that into account
with monetary policy.

37:24.286 --> 37:26.456 align:middle
How, specifically, will
you take that into account?

37:26.456 --> 37:26.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:27.426 --> 37:30.246 align:middle
Yes, it's hard to say, because
there's so many other variables.

37:30.246 --> 37:34.416 align:middle
Ultimately, there are
many variables to be taken

37:34.416 --> 37:38.076 align:middle
into account at any given time,
but that's part of our mandate.

37:38.416 --> 37:40.316 align:middle
Stable prices is
half of our mandate,

37:40.906 --> 37:42.236 align:middle
and we've defined
that as 2 percent.

37:42.236 --> 37:45.776 align:middle
So we would be concerned, and
we'd take it into account.

37:46.186 --> 37:46.696 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

37:47.026 --> 37:49.486 align:middle
So a cut in interest
rates would be possible?

37:49.486 --> 37:49.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:49.716 --> 37:55.516 align:middle
I can't really be any more
specific than what I've said.

37:57.566 --> 37:59.116 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

37:59.356 --> 38:00.866 align:middle
Hi, Jean Yung with Market News.

38:01.026 --> 38:04.606 align:middle
If the fed funds rate keeps
rising, do you see room

38:04.606 --> 38:07.336 align:middle
for another IOER adjustment?

38:07.596 --> 38:11.396 align:middle
And then, can you speak to
any other strategies or tools

38:11.806 --> 38:14.156 align:middle
that might be useful
for keeping a ceiling

38:14.156 --> 38:15.616 align:middle
on short-term interest rates?

38:16.216 --> 38:17.846 align:middle
There have been other
ideas floated,

38:17.846 --> 38:20.236 align:middle
like the standing repo facility

38:20.236 --> 38:23.036 align:middle
and targeting a different
benchmark rate.

38:23.876 --> 38:24.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:24.816 --> 38:30.046 align:middle
If we need to, and as
needed, we will use our tools

38:30.716 --> 38:35.646 align:middle
to keep the federal funds rate
somewhere in the target range.

38:35.756 --> 38:36.246 align:middle
We'll do that.

38:36.616 --> 38:38.706 align:middle
Don't expect to need to do
it again, but we don't know.

38:38.706 --> 38:44.396 align:middle
I mean, we're-with-the
balance sheet is now-the size

38:44.396 --> 38:46.576 align:middle
of it is going to be driven
by demand for liabilities,

38:46.576 --> 38:48.446 align:middle
principally reserves,
as I mentioned.

38:49.056 --> 38:50.916 align:middle
And we're right at that
point where we're starting

38:50.916 --> 38:53.866 align:middle
to learn more and more
about what the real demand

38:53.866 --> 38:56.376 align:middle
for reserves is over
the next few months.

38:56.376 --> 38:58.876 align:middle
And so I-there is no
template for this.

38:58.876 --> 38:59.776 align:middle
There's no roadmap.

38:59.776 --> 39:01.016 align:middle
We just have to do it,

39:01.016 --> 39:02.926 align:middle
and that's why we're
moving so very gradually.

39:02.926 --> 39:07.096 align:middle
It's why we tapered the
roll-off to only $15 billion

39:07.096 --> 39:08.376 align:middle
in Treasuries per month.

39:08.376 --> 39:12.006 align:middle
Effective, I guess, today,
we're cutting the roll-off rate

39:12.006 --> 39:16.736 align:middle
for Treasuries in half just
because we want to take our time

39:16.736 --> 39:17.736 align:middle
and move gradually here.

39:17.736 --> 39:19.896 align:middle
So that's that.

39:19.896 --> 39:24.226 align:middle
In terms of other tools,
we're actually-I'm sure

39:24.226 --> 39:26.346 align:middle
at an upcoming meeting
we will be looking

39:26.346 --> 39:28.306 align:middle
at the idea of a repo facility.

39:28.306 --> 39:31.356 align:middle
I don't have any presupposition
that it's something we would do,

39:31.356 --> 39:33.536 align:middle
but we'll be taking a look at it

39:33.536 --> 39:36.976 align:middle
as a possible addition
to our toolbox.

39:36.976 --> 39:40.526 align:middle
Again, I wouldn't say-it
would just be a way for us

39:40.626 --> 39:42.996 align:middle
to do what we do, which is
to do a deep dive on it,

39:42.996 --> 39:46.026 align:middle
think carefully about it,
look at the pros and cons,

39:46.026 --> 39:48.566 align:middle
look at the different possible
ways to do it, and then go away

39:48.566 --> 39:49.676 align:middle
and think about it
for a little while.

39:49.726 --> 39:53.116 align:middle
And then probably come
back and make a decision.

39:53.116 --> 39:54.186 align:middle
But that's something we'll do

39:54.186 --> 39:55.696 align:middle
at an upcoming meeting,
I would imagine.

39:56.986 --> 39:58.566 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

39:59.676 --> 40:03.126 align:middle
Hannah Lang, American Banker.

40:03.186 --> 40:05.146 align:middle
On the regulatory side, the Fed

40:05.146 --> 40:08.296 align:middle
and other bank regulators are
said to be weighing all options

40:08.296 --> 40:10.736 align:middle
for retooling a proposal
to revise the Volcker rule.

40:11.176 --> 40:14.286 align:middle
Can you give any indication how
close the agencies are to coming

40:14.286 --> 40:17.106 align:middle
to a solution and what that
solution might look like,

40:17.106 --> 40:19.646 align:middle
whether it would be starting
from scratch entirely

40:19.646 --> 40:21.706 align:middle
or making changes to
the original proposal?

40:22.246 --> 40:22.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:22.596 --> 40:26.016 align:middle
You know, we put out a proposal
on Volcker some time ago,

40:26.016 --> 40:28.156 align:middle
and we got a lot of comments,

40:28.156 --> 40:29.596 align:middle
and we're reviewing
them carefully.

40:29.966 --> 40:31.336 align:middle
I really don't have
a lot for you.

40:31.336 --> 40:33.016 align:middle
I know they're making
good progress.

40:33.016 --> 40:34.466 align:middle
I don't really have
a date though.

40:34.826 --> 40:36.996 align:middle
And in terms of what
all it's going to be,

40:37.146 --> 40:41.806 align:middle
it's not something I can
say with any certainty yet.

40:42.906 --> 40:44.416 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

40:46.136 --> 40:49.496 align:middle
Hi there. Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

40:49.496 --> 40:52.886 align:middle
So given what you've said about
the expectations for consumption

40:52.886 --> 40:55.336 align:middle
and also business fixed
investment perhaps bouncing back

40:55.696 --> 40:58.916 align:middle
in the next GDP reading,
I'm wondering if you think

40:58.916 --> 40:59.736 align:middle
that continued growth

40:59.736 --> 41:02.386 align:middle
in economic activity
might show some sort

41:02.386 --> 41:05.606 align:middle
of underlying fundamental that
could flag another overheating.

41:05.606 --> 41:08.096 align:middle
For example, the median
projection in the March meeting

41:08.096 --> 41:11.876 align:middle
for GDP growth for 2019 was
2.1, so I'm just kind of curious

41:11.876 --> 41:13.826 align:middle
about what you see going
forward if we continue

41:13.826 --> 41:15.296 align:middle
to see strong GDP numbers.

41:15.296 --> 41:16.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:16.226 --> 41:17.836 align:middle
Well, we don't see
any evidence at all

41:17.836 --> 41:19.216 align:middle
of overheating, for one thing.

41:19.556 --> 41:24.336 align:middle
We see inflation below 2 percent
now-as I mentioned, pretty close

41:24.336 --> 41:25.946 align:middle
to 2 percent for
most of last year.

41:26.906 --> 41:29.276 align:middle
So, really, no signs
of overheating.

41:29.276 --> 41:30.736 align:middle
If you look at the labor market,

41:30.976 --> 41:34.036 align:middle
for a long time now there
have been anecdotal reports

41:34.036 --> 41:35.666 align:middle
of labor shortages
and difficulty

41:35.666 --> 41:37.526 align:middle
in finding skilled labor
and that kind of thing.

41:37.986 --> 41:41.626 align:middle
Nonetheless, you have
very strong job creation,

41:41.626 --> 41:46.666 align:middle
and you have wages moving up
at a rate that is appropriate,

41:46.666 --> 41:48.926 align:middle
given inflation and
given productivity,

41:48.976 --> 41:51.846 align:middle
but not at all signaling
any overheating at all.

41:51.846 --> 41:54.696 align:middle
It's not higher than that rate

41:54.826 --> 41:57.266 align:middle
that would incorporate both
inflation and productivity.

41:57.266 --> 42:01.136 align:middle
So, really not seeing signs
of overheating at the moment.

42:02.336 --> 42:07.406 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

42:09.026 --> 42:12.606 align:middle
Are wages ever going to get back
above 4 percent in this cycle?

42:12.816 --> 42:16.666 align:middle
Can you kind of give us a read
on-but more substantively,

42:16.666 --> 42:17.616 align:middle
can you give us a read

42:17.616 --> 42:20.436 align:middle
on how the Committee
views productivity growth,

42:20.626 --> 42:22.726 align:middle
if it's accelerating enough?

42:22.896 --> 42:24.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:24.226 --> 42:28.886 align:middle
Yes-in terms of wages getting
over 4 percent, wages have moved

42:28.886 --> 42:33.066 align:middle
up pretty steadily over the last
five years and are now-wages

42:33.066 --> 42:35.966 align:middle
and benefits are now
between 3 and 3½ percent.

42:36.646 --> 42:40.816 align:middle
Just for the last couple
of years, the biggest part

42:40.816 --> 42:43.746 align:middle
of the gains have come for
people at the lowest end

42:43.746 --> 42:45.166 align:middle
of compensation and education,

42:45.166 --> 42:48.326 align:middle
which is kind of
a welcome thing.

42:48.896 --> 42:50.276 align:middle
You mentioned productivity.

42:50.276 --> 42:55.046 align:middle
So productivity is really
very difficult to predict.

42:55.046 --> 42:57.236 align:middle
No one has been able to
predict it successfully,

42:57.466 --> 42:58.776 align:middle
so I won't really try.

42:58.776 --> 43:01.506 align:middle
But I will say this:
Productivity was very,

43:01.506 --> 43:04.876 align:middle
very low in the wake of the
crisis for six or seven years.

43:05.326 --> 43:08.316 align:middle
Last year, we had 1.9
percent productivity,

43:08.316 --> 43:09.306 align:middle
which is much higher.

43:09.976 --> 43:12.366 align:middle
I don't know if that
level can be sustained,

43:12.366 --> 43:16.386 align:middle
but we really-it's
driven, to some extent,

43:16.436 --> 43:19.716 align:middle
by technological developments
and, really, the diffusion

43:19.716 --> 43:22.116 align:middle
of technology through
the economy,

43:22.186 --> 43:23.726 align:middle
and it's very hard to predict.

43:23.726 --> 43:25.216 align:middle
So I think it's positive.

43:25.216 --> 43:28.236 align:middle
In fact, I'll mention, if we're
talking about the supply side,

43:28.696 --> 43:29.786 align:middle
labor force participation.

43:29.856 --> 43:33.436 align:middle
Really, there has been a
significant positive supply-side

43:33.436 --> 43:37.226 align:middle
development over the last year
and a half between the uptick

43:37.226 --> 43:40.986 align:middle
in labor force participation,
which goes back several years,

43:40.986 --> 43:42.576 align:middle
but also productivity.

43:42.606 --> 43:44.516 align:middle
And that does suggest
more room to grow.

43:44.516 --> 43:47.116 align:middle
It suggests that a less
tight economy, a less,

43:48.036 --> 43:52.956 align:middle
you know-may be part of the
explanation for lower inflation.

43:55.916 --> 43:57.356 align:middle
Thanks very much.

43:58.166 --> 44:30.146 align:middle
